title: Diagnostics change to a disjoint set when the same plugin is found under two --plugins-dir roots
updated_at: "2026-09-09T03:09:14Z"
---
When the same plugin is discoverable through two `--plugins-dir` roots (a staged copy
plus its source checkout), `check` produces a completely different — and disjoint —
set of errors for identical source.

## Repro

`~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins/Editor` is the staged copy of
`~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-editor-plus-plus` (same `siteid = 377`, same version).

One root at a time — 2 errors (see B182, both false positives but at least stable):

    $ openplanet-lsp check --plugins-dir ~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins .
    src/Components/Map/LmQualityTuner.as:1285:30: error: Only object types that support object handles can use &inout...
    src/FromML.as:137:31: error: Only object types that support object handles can use &inout...
    35 diagnostics (2 errors, 33 warnings)

    $ openplanet-lsp check --plugins-dir ~/src/openplanet/my-plugins .
    (identical 2 errors)

Both roots together — the two `&inout` errors vanish and three unrelated
overload-resolution errors appear instead:

    $ openplanet-lsp check --plugins-dir ~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins --plugins-dir ~/src/openplanet/my-plugins .
    src/Components/Inventory/Browser.as:239:80: error: No matching signatures to 'UI::BeginChild(string, vec2, const bool, UI::WindowFlags)'
    src/Components/Map/Map_EditProps.as:134:72: error: No matching signatures to 'UI::InputTextMultiline(const string, wstring, vec2)'
    src/UI/UI_TabGroup.as:168:63: error: No matching signatures to 'UI::BeginChild(<error>, vec2, UI::ChildFlags)'
    36 diagnostics (3 errors, 33 warnings)

Each configuration is deterministic across repeated runs; they just disagree with
each other. All five errors are false positives (the plugin builds and loads on
Openplanet 1.29.14). See B184 for the three UI ones.

## Notes / suspicion

The plugin appearing twice under different roots looks like it corrupts symbol or
typedb resolution — a duplicate module shadowing the builtin `UI::` overload set
would explain both the appearance of the UI errors and the disappearance of the
`&inout` ones (analysis taking a different path / bailing earlier).

Whatever the root cause, adding a plugins-dir should only ever *add* resolvable
dependencies. It should never change diagnostics for the plugin under check into a
disjoint set, and a duplicate plugin id across roots should probably be deduped
(prefer the checked source) or warned about.

